'''Gillette''' is an American [[brand]] of [[safety razor]]s and other [[personal care]] products including shaving supplies, owned by the multi-national corporation [[Procter & Gamble]] (P&G). Based in [[Boston]], Massachusetts, United States, it was owned by '''The Gillette Company''', a supplier of products under various brands until that company merged into P&G in 2005. The Gillette Company was founded by [[King C. Gillette]] in 1901 as a safety razor [[manufacturer]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|url=https://money.cnn.com/magazines/fsb/fsb_archive/2003/04/01/341005/|title=Gillette in His Early Days the Inventor of the Razor and the Company He Build Survived Many Close Shaves with Financial Ruin. But His Fame Never Translated into a Personal Fortune|date=April 1, 2003|website=money.cnn.com|access-date=2019-10-23|archive-date=2020-11-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125151215/https://money.cnn.com/magazines/fsb/fsb_archive/2003/04/01/341005/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Under the leadership of [[Colman M. Mockler Jr.]] as CEO from 1975 to 1991,<ref name="Fowler">{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1991/01/26/obituaries/colman-m-mockler-jr-dies-at-61-gillette-s-top-executive-since-70-s.html|title=Colman M. Mockler Jr. Dies at 61, Gillette's Top Executive Since 70's|last=Fowler|first=Glenn|date=1991-01-26|work=The New York Times|access-date=2019-09-18|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=2020-09-04|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200904111210/https://www.nytimes.com/1991/01/26/obituaries/colman-m-mockler-jr-dies-at-61-gillette-s-top-executive-since-70-s.html|url-status=live}}</ref> the company was the target of multiple [[takeover]] attempts from [[Ronald Perelman]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1991/01/25/Gillette-chairman-dies-of-heart-attack/8480664779600/|title=Gillette chairman dies of heart attack|website=UPI|language=en|access-date=2019-09-18|archive-date=2020-08-09|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200809043556/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1991/01/25/Gillette-chairman-dies-of-heart-attack/8480664779600/|url-status=live}}</ref> and Coniston Partners.<ref name="Fowler"/> In January 2005, Procter & Gamble announced plans to merge with the Gillette Company.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/procter-gamble-acquires-gillette/|title=Procter & Gamble Acquires Gillette|website=www.cbsnews.com|date=28 January 2005 |language=en-US|access-date=2019-09-18|archive-date=2020-08-03|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200803084556/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/procter-gamble-acquires-gillette/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The Gillette Company's assets were incorporated into a P&G unit known internally as "Global Gillette". In July 2007, Global Gillette was dissolved and incorporated into Procter & Gamble's other two main divisions, Procter & Gamble Beauty and Procter & Gamble Household Care.<ref>{{cite web |title=P&G 2007 Annual Report |url=https://s1.q4cdn.com/695946674/files/doc_financials/2007/96465dd0-087a-83bb-cf05-acf65bc36b2a.pdf |publisher=Procter & Gamble |access-date=10 November 2023 |pages=32–33 |date=23 August 2007 |quote=The businesses that previously comprised the Gillette GBU will now be included within the Beauty and Household Care GBUs.}}</ref> Gillette's brands and products were divided between the two accordingly.  The Gillette R&D center in Boston, Massachusetts, and the Gillette South Boston Manufacturing Center (known as "Gillette World Shaving Headquarters"), still exist as functional working locations under the Procter & Gamble-owned Gillette brand name.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|url=http://news.gillette.com/about/history|title=Gillette history|website=gillette.com|access-date=August 22, 2014|archive-date=August 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190804033554/https://news.gillette.com/about/history|url-status=dead}}</ref> Gillette's subsidiaries [[Braun (company)|Braun]] and [[Oral-B]], among others, have also been retained by P&G.

== History ==
=== Inception and early years (1895 - 1930) ===
{{multiple image
| total_width = 450
| image1 = King Camp Gillette.jpg
| image2 = William E Nickerson.jpg
| image3 = John Joyce.jpg
| footer = The key figures of the Gillette Safety Razor Company's first years, from left to right: [[King Camp Gillette]], William Emery Nickerson, and John Joyce
}}
[[File:Vintage Advert for the Milady Decollete Gillette Razor - 1916 (5248628800).jpg|thumb|right|1915–16 advert for the ''Milady Décolleté Gillette'', first safety razor marketed exclusively for women]]

The Gillette company and brand originate from the late 19th century when salesman and inventor [[King Camp Gillette]] came up with the idea of a [[safety razor]] that used disposable blades. Safety razors at the time were essentially short pieces of a [[straight razor]] clamped to a holder. The blade had to be [[Razor strop|stropped]] before each shave and after a time needed to be [[Sharpening|honed]] by a cutler.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=5}} Gillette's invention was inspired by his mentor at [[Crown Cork & Seal Company]], [[William Painter (inventor)|William Painter]], who had invented the [[Crown cork]]. Painter encouraged Gillette to come up with something that, like the Crown cork, could be thrown away once used.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=7–8}}{{sfn|Gillette|1918}}

While Gillette came up with the idea in 1895, developing the concept into a working model and drawings that could be submitted to the [[United States Patent and Trademark Office|Patent Office]] took six years. Gillette had trouble finding anyone capable of developing a method to manufacture blades from thin [[sheet steel]], but finally found [[William Emery Nickerson]], an [[MIT]] graduate with a degree in chemistry. Gillette and other members of the project founded The American Safety Razor Company on September 28, 1901. The company had issues getting funding until Gillette's old friend John Joyce invested the necessary amount for the company to begin manufacturing.<ref name="smithsonian-blade"/>{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=7–8}}{{sfn|Gillette|1918}} Production began slowly in 1903, but the following year Nickerson succeeded in building a new blade grinding machine to relieve bottlenecked production. During its first year of operation, the company had sold 51 razors and 168 blades, but the second year saw sales rise to 90,884 razors and 123,648 blades. The company was renamed to the Gillette Safety Razor Company in 1904 and it quickly began to expand outside the United States. In 1905 the company opened a sales office in London and a blade manufacturing plant in Paris, and by 1906 Gillette had a blade plant in Canada, a sales operation in Mexico, and a European distribution network that sold  in many nations, including Russia.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=7–8}}<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HZLtCQAAQBAJ&q=American+Safety+Razor+Company|title=The Advertising Age Encyclopedia of Advertising|first1=John|last1=McDonough|first2=Karen|last2=Egolf|date=June 18, 2015|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781135949068|via=Google Books }}</ref>

Due to its premium pricing strategy, the Gillette Safety Razor Company's razor and blade unit sales grew at a modest pace from 1908 to 1916. Disposable razor blades still were not a true mass-market product, and [[wikt:Special:Search/barbershop|barbershops]] and self-shaving with a straight razor were still popular methods of grooming. Among the general U.S. population, a two-day stubble was not uncommon. This changed once the United States declared war on the [[Central Powers]] in 1917; military regulations required every soldier to provide their own shaving kit, and Gillette's compact kit with disposable blades outsold competitors whose razors required stropping. Gillette marketed its razor by designing a military-only case decorated with U.S. Army and Navy insignia and in 1917 the company sold 1.1 million razors.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=18–20}}

[[File:Vintage Gillette Khaki U.S. Army Safety Razor Set, Model 102 Razor, Serial No. G504335, Made In USA For Use By Soldiers In World War I, Marked Property U.S. Army, Circa 1918.jpg|thumb|upright=1.1|The Khaki Set, the safety razor set produced by Gillette for the U.S. Army during the First World War<ref name="WWI-service-sets">{{Cite web|url=https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/search/object/nmah_1153449|title=Gillette U.S. Service Razor Set|website=National Museum of American History|access-date=2019-10-27|archive-date=2020-11-11|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201111170935/https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/search/object/nmah_1153449|url-status=live}}</ref>]]

In 1918, the U.S. military began issuing Gillette shaving kits to every U.S. serviceman. Gillette's sales rose to 3.5 million razors and 32 million blades. As a consequence, millions of servicemen got accustomed to daily shaving using Gillette's razor. After the war, Gillette utilized this in its domestic marketing and used advertising to reinforce the habit acquired during the war.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=18–20}}

Gillette's original razor patent was due to expire in November 1921 and to stay ahead of an upcoming competition, the company introduced the New Improved Gillette Safety Razor in spring 1921 and switched to the [[Razor and blades model|razor and blades pricing structure]] the company is known for today. While the New Improved razor was sold for $5 ({{Inflation|US|5|1921|fmt=eq}}) – the selling price of the previous razor – the original razor was renamed to the Old Type and sold in inexpensive packaging as "Brownies" for $1 ({{Inflation|US|1|1921|fmt=eq}}). While some Old Type models were still sold in various kinds of packaging for an average price of $3.50, the Brownie razors made a Gillette much more affordable for the average person and expanded the company's blade market significantly. From 1917 to 1925, Gillette's unit sales increased tenfold. The company also expanded its overseas operations right after the war by opening a manufacturing plant in [[Slough]], near London, to build New Improved razors, and setting up dozens of offices and subsidiaries in Europe and other parts of the world.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=20–21}}

Gillette experienced a setback at the end of the 1920s as its competitor AutoStrop sued for patent infringement. The case was settled out of court with Gillette agreeing to buy out AutoStrop for 310,000 [[non-voting shares]]. However, before the deal went through, it was revealed in an audit that Gillette had been overstating its sales and profits by $12 million over a five-year period and giving bonuses to its executives based on these numbers. AutoStrop still agreed to the buyout but instead demanded a large amount of [[preferred stock]] with voting rights. The merger was announced on October 16, 1930, and gave AutoStrop's owner [[Henry Gaisman]] controlling interest in Gillette.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=29–30}}

=== 1930s and the Second World War ===
[[File:1930's Gillette Razor and Packaging rightcrop.png|thumb|upright=1.1|A 1930s Gillette One-Piece Tech razor and a pack of Blue Blades]]

[[The Great Depression]] weakened Gillette's position in the market significantly. The company had fallen behind its competitors in blade manufacturing technology in the 1920s and had let [[quality control]] slip while over-stretching its production equipment in order to hurry a new Kroman razor and stainless steel blade{{sfn|Adams|1978|p=141}} to market in 1930. In 1932, Gillette apologized for the reduction in blade quality, withdrew the Kroman blade, and introduced the Blue Blade (initially called the Blue Super Blade) as its replacement.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=29–33}} Other Gillette introductions during the Depression years were the Brushless Shaving Cream and an electric shaver that was soon discontinued due to modest sales.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=34}}

In 1938 Gillette introduced the Thin Blade, which was cheaper and about half the weight of the Blue Blade, even though it cost almost as much to manufacture. The Thin Blade became more popular than the Blue Blade for several years during the Second World War due to high demand of low-cost products and the shortage of carbon steel.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=35}} Beginning in 1939, Gillette began investing significant amounts on advertising in sports events after its advertising in the [[World Series]] increased sales more than double the company had expected.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=36–39}} It eventually sponsored a radio program, the ''[[Gillette Cavalcade of Sports]]'', which would move to television as well as that medium expanded in the late 1940s.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Cross |first1=Mary |title=A Century of American Icons: 100 Products and Slogans from the 20th-Century Consumer Culture |date=2002 |publisher=Greenwood Press |isbn=978-0313314810  |access-date=4 September 2020 |url=https://archive.org/details/centuryofamerica00cros/page/101/ |pages=101–02}}</ref> While the ''Cavalcade'' aired many of the notable sporting events of the time (the [[Kentucky Derby]], college football bowl games, and baseball, amongst others) it became most famous for its [[professional boxing]] broadcasts.

Though competition hit Gillette hard in the domestic market during the Great Depression, overseas operations helped keep the company afloat. In 1935 more than half of Gillette's earnings came from foreign operations and in 1938 – the worst of the Depression years for Gillette, with a mere 18 percent market share – nearly all of the company's $2.9 million earnings came from outside the United States. AutoStrop's Brazilian factory allowed Gillette to start expanding into the Latin America. In England the Gillette and AutoStrop operations were combined under the Gillette name, where the company built a new blade manufacturing plant in London. In 1937, Gillette's Berlin factory produced 40 percent of Germany's 900 million blades and retained a 65 to 75 percent share of the German blade market.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=34–35}}

The Second World War reduced Gillette's blade production both domestically and internationally. As a result of the war, many markets were closed off, German and Japanese forces [[Nationalization#Expropriation|expropriated]] the company's plants and property, and Gillette's plants in Boston and London were partially converted for weapons production. In 1942, the [[War Production Board]] ordered Gillette to dedicate its entire razor production and most blade production to the U.S. military. By the end of the war, servicemen had been issued 12.5 million razors and 1.5 billion blades. Gillette also assisted the U.S. Army in military intelligence by producing copies of German razor blades for secret agents venturing behind German lines so that their identities wouldn't be compromised by their shaving equipment. The company also manufactured razors that concealed money and escape maps in their handles, and magnetic double-edge blades that [[prisoners of war]] could use as a compass.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=39–40}}

=== Recovery from the war and diversification (1946 - 1959)===

During the post-war years, Gillette began to quickly ramp up production by modernizing its major manufacturing plants in the United States and England, expanding the capacity of several foreign plants, and re-opening plants closed during the war. The company opened a new plant in Switzerland and began manufacturing blades in Mexico City. Sales rose to $50 million in 1946 and in 1947 Gillette sold a billion blades. By 1950, Gillette's share of the U.S. blade market had climbed to 50 percent.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=40–45}} During the 1950s, the company updated and in some cases moved some of its older European factories: the Paris factory, for example, was moved to [[Annecy]].{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=61}}

[[File:Vintage Gillette Red Tip Super Speed DE Safety Razor, Twist-To-Open, Made In USA, Date Code D-1 (1958) (30521266125).jpg|thumb|upright=1.1|A 1958 Gillette Super Speed razor and a blade dispenser]]

In 1947 Gillette introduced the Gillette Super Speed razor and along with it the Speed-pak blade dispenser the company had developed during the war. The dispenser allowed the blade to be slid out of the dispenser into the razor without danger of touching the sharp edge. It also had a compartment for holding used blades.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=44–45}}{{sfn|Waits|2014|p=466}}

In 1948 Gillette bought the home [[Perm (hairstyle)|permanent]] kit manufacturer The Toni Company{{sfn|McKibben|1998|loc=The legend of the "Which twin has the Toni?" advert between pages 52 and 53}} and later expanded into other feminine products such as shampoos and hair sprays. In 1955 the company bought the ballpoint pen manufacturer [[Paper Mate]]{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=44–45}}<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://money.cnn.com/magazines/fsb/fsb_archive/2003/04/01/341006/index.htm|title=A Permanent Contribution When Gillette bought a hot startup in an effort to diversify, it got something even better: the entrepreneur behind the product|date=April 1, 2003|website=money.cnn.com|access-date=August 3, 2020|archive-date=July 2, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090702105152/http://money.cnn.com/magazines/fsb/fsb_archive/2003/04/01/341006/index.htm|url-status=dead}}</ref> and in 1960 it introduced Right Guard aerosol deodorant. Gillette bought the disposable hospital supplies manufacturer Sterilon Corporation in 1962.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=54–55}}

Television advertising played a big part in Gillette's post-war growth in the United States. The company began TV advertising in 1944 and in 1950 it spent $6 million to acquire exclusive sponsorship rights to the World Series for six years. By the mid-1950s, 85 percent of Gillette's advertising budget was used for television advertising. The company also advertised the Toni product line by sponsoring the TV show [[Arthur Godfrey and His Friends]] and the 1958 [[Miss America]] contest and its winner.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=49–52}}<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://adage.com/article/adage-encyclopedia/gillette/98674/|title=Gillette Co. &#124; AdAge Encyclopedia of Advertising – Advertising Age|date=May 20, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150520022102/http://adage.com/article/adage-encyclopedia/gillette/98674/|archive-date=2015-05-20}}</ref>

[[File:Gillette Ad How Are Ya Fixed For Blades.webm|left|thumb|upright=1.1|1950s TV commercial for Gillette's Blue Blades]]

Although Gillette's immediate priority after the war was satisfying U.S. demand and later diversifying its domestic business, the company pursued expansion in foreign markets that showed potential for growth, such as Latin America and Asia. However, the [[Cold War]] restricted Gillette's operations in many parts of the world and closed entire markets the company would have otherwise entered in Russia, China, Eastern Europe, Near East, Cuba, and parts of Asia. More and more countries demanded local ownership for foreign enterprise in exchange for continued operation or entry into their markets. Outside the U.S. and European markets, Gillette spent time and money building manufacturing facilities and distribution networks in anticipation that the markets would eventually be opened up and nationalistic restrictions lifted. Some of Gillette's joint ventures included a 40 percent Gillette 60 percent Malaysian mini-plant operation that began production in 1970, and an Iranian manufacturing plant with 51 percent government ownership. The Iran plant was one of Gillette's largest and most modern factories until the [[Iranian Revolution]] of 1979 when [[Ayatollah Khomeini]] rose into power and American businesses were targeted as enemies of the new government, forcing Gillette to abandon the operation and withdraw from the country.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=61–66}}

=== Super Blue to disposables (1960 - 1980)===

In 1960, Gillette introduced the Super Blue blade, the company's first coated blade, and the first significantly improved razor blade since the Blue Blade of the 1930s. The new blade was coated with silicone and in Gillette's laboratory testing produced much more comfortable and close shaves by reducing the blade's adhesion to whiskers. Super Blue was a success and sold more than the Blue Blade and Thin Blade combined. By the end of 1961, Gillette's double edge blade market share had risen to 90 percent and the company held a total razor blade market share of 70 percent.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=53–54}}

In 1962, roughly two years after the introduction of the Super Blue blade, [[Wilkinson Sword]] introduced a coated razor blade made from [[stainless steel]]. According to users, the blade stayed sharp about three times longer than the best carbon steel blades – including Gillette's. Wilkinson's introduction took Gillette by surprise and the company struggled to respond as its smaller rivals, [[Schick (razors)|Schick]] and the [[American Safety Razor Company]], came out with their own stainless steel blade. However, during the development of the silicon coating for the Super Blue blade, Gillette had also discovered the method of producing coated stainless steel blades that Wilkinson Sword was using and managed to patent it before Wilkinson did. The English company ended up having to pay royalties to Gillette for the stainless steel blades it brought to market.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=56–58}}

Gillette hesitated in bringing its own stainless steel blades to market as Super Blue had been a huge success and replacing it with a longer-lasting blade would have reduced profits. The company had originally planned to enjoy high profits from the Super Blue while developing a better stainless steel alloy. Gillette eventually brought the Gillette Stainless blade to market in August 1963, about a year after Wilkinson's stainless blades. As a result of the affair, Gillette's share of the double-edge blade market dropped from 90 percent to about 70 percent.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=56–58}}

Two years after the introduction of the Gillette Stainless blades, the company brought out the Super Stainless blades – known in Europe as Super Silver – that were made from an improved steel alloy. {{anchor|Techmatic}}Gillette also introduced the Techmatic, a new type of razor that used a continuous spool of stainless blade housed in a plastic cartridge.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=58}}{{sfn|Waits|2014|p=468}}

The success of the coated Super Blue blades marked the start of a period when chemistry became as important as metallurgy in Gillette's blade manufacturing. The Super Blue's coating was a result of teamwork between the Gillette's British and American scientists.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=53}} As a result of the Wilkinson ordeal, Gillette's then-chairman Carl Gilbert increased the company's spending on research and development facilities in the U.S., championed the building of a research facility in [[Rockville, Maryland]], and encouraged further expansion of R&D activities in England.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=59}}

{{multiple image
| direction = vertical
| total_width = 260
| image1 = Gillette Atra patent illustration.png
| image2 = Vintage Gillette Deluxe Trac II Razor, Circa 1972 (14571948670).jpg
| footer = Patent drawing of the abandoned Atra razor and a picture of a Deluxe model Trac II razor
}}

The development of Gillette's first twin-blade razor began in early 1964 in the company's Reading laboratories in England when a new employee, Norman C. Welsh, experimented with tandem blades and discovered what he called the "hysteresis effect"; a blade pulling the whisker out of the hair follicle before cutting it, and enabling a second blade to cut the whisker even shorter before it retracted back into the follicle. For six years afterwards, Welsh and his colleagues worked on a means of utilizing the hysteresis effect, almost exclusively concentrating on what would later be known as the Atra twin-blade system. The Atra razor featured two blades set in a plastic cartridge with edges that faced each other. Using the razor required the user to move it in an up-and-down scrubbing motion, and whiskers were cut on both the up and down strokes. Another twin-blade system with blades set in tandem, codenamed "Rex", also existed, but it had too many technical problems and was behind Atra in development.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=67–68}}

In consumer tests, the Atra razor had outperformed existing razor systems, but Gillette's marketing executives feared the razor would meet resistance among shavers due to the unfamiliar scrubbing motion required to use it. Even though the Atra project was so far along in mid-1970 that packaging and production machinery was nearly ready for a full market introduction, Gillette decided to start a development drive to finish Rex instead as it did not require learning a new way to shave. The project succeeded, Atra was abandoned, and Gillette announced the first twin-blade razor – now renamed to Trac II – in the fall of 1971. The Trac II captured the premium shaving market and came out in time to counter Wilkinson Sword's Bonded Blade system that utilized single-blade cartridges.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=67–69}}

In 1974, the French [[Société Bic]] introduced the world's first [[Razor#Disposable safety razors|disposable razor]]. The razor was first brought to the market in Greece, where it sold well, after which it was introduced to Italy and many other European countries. Gillette hurried to develop its own disposable before Bic could bring its razor to the United States. Gillette designed a single-blade razor similar to Bic's but soon abandoned the concept in favor of a razor that was essentially a Trac II cartridge molded into a blue plastic handle. Gillette introduced this disposable as the Good News in 1976, about a year before Bic's razor reached the United States, and managed to establish market leadership once Bic and other competitors came to market. Good News was released under various names in Europe and was equally and sometimes even more successful than Bic's razor. Gillette quickly brought its razor to markets Bic hadn't yet reached, such as Latin America where the razor was known as Prestobarba.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=97–98}}

In Latin America, Gillette used a so-called [[Cannibalization (marketing)|cannibalization strategy]] by selling the razor in several [[market segments]]: along with the heavily advertised Prestobarba, the razor was also sold under different names – such as Permasharp II in Mexico and Probak II in Brazil – and sold for at least 10 to 15 percent less. The less expensive variants were differentiated from the blue Prestobarba by manufacturing them from yellow plastic with handle less ergonomically comfortable, and in addition, they were not advertised or eventually had [[counterpropaganda|negative marketing]]<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dkTE_S0mr3c Vitor Belfort e Lutadores UFC: Vai Amarelar] - Advertising where the Probak II product from Gillette's low-cost disposable razors line is treated with [[counterpropaganda|negative marketing]] in order to promote the Prestobarba brand, a product sold at a higher price by Gillette.</ref> aimed at promoting the sale of the most profitable products in Gillette's razors line. The strategy was successful and later market arrivals were unable to gain a major foothold. Once the approach proved to be a successful one, Gillette's subsidiaries in Russia, Poland, and multiple Asian and Near Eastern markets began utilizing the same strategy.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=336–37}}

While Gillette managed to retain market leadership against Bic and other competitors, the popularity of disposable razors, their higher production cost compared to cartridges, and price competition eroded the company's profits. Gillette had at first hoped that disposables would take no more than 10 percent of the total razor and blades market, but by 1980, disposables accounted for more than 27 percent of the world shaving market in terms of unit sales, and 22 percent of total revenue.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=98–99}}<ref>[https://www.casrilanka.com/casl/images/stories/EDBA/03%20gillette%20and%20the%20mengcos%20wet%20shaving%20market%20%20business%20case%20%20team%2003%202018.pdf Gillette and the men's wet-shaving market] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200809060100/https://www.casrilanka.com/casl/images/stories/EDBA/03%20gillette%20and%20the%20mengcos%20wet%20shaving%20market%20%20business%20case%20%20team%2003%202018.pdf |date=2020-08-09 }}, p. 2</ref> John W. Symons began steering Gillette into a different direction after becoming the director of Gillette's European Sales Group in 1979. Despite Gillette's strong sales and a large share of the European razor and blades market – 70 percent, which was higher than in the United States – cash flow was declining. In Symons's view, the issue was Gillette's attempt to compete with Bic in the disposables market, which was eating into the sales of its more profitable cartridge razors. Symons reduced the marketing budget of disposables in Europe and hired the advertising agency [[BBDO]]'s London branch to create an ad campaign to make Gillette's blade and razor systems – such as Contour – more desirable in the eyes of men. The new marketing strategy, combined with cutting costs and centralizing production increased profits. In 1985, Gillette's profits in the European market were $96 million, while two years previously they were $77 million.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|pp=114–17}}

In 1980, Gillette introduced Atra – known in Europe as Contour – a twin-blade razor with a pivoting head. The razor became a best-seller in the United States during its first year and eventually became a market leader in Europe.{{sfn|McKibben|1998|p=96}}

=== Acquisitions and takeover attempts (1984 - present)===
[[File:Gillette old.png|thumb|The logo of Gillette used from 1989 to 2008]]

In 1984, Gillette agreed to acquire Oral-B Laboratories from dental care company Cooper Laboratories for $188.5 million in cash.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gillette Will Buy Cooper's Oral-B |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1984/04/05/business/gillette-will-buy-cooper-s-oral-b.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=April 5, 1984 |archive-date=February 15, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180215023524/http://www.nytimes.com/1984/04/05/business/gillette-will-buy-cooper-s-oral-b.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Revlon|Revlon Group]]'s [[Ronald Perelman]] offered to purchase 86.1 percent of Gillette for $3.8 billion in 1986, valuing the company at $4.1 billion.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cole |first1=Robert J. |title=Gillette Soars $10 as Wall Street Expects Fight with Revlon |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1986/11/15/business/gillette-soars-10-as-wall-street-expects-fight-with-revlon.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=November 15, 1986 |archive-date=February 3, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180203100708/http://www.nytimes.com/1986/11/15/business/gillette-soars-10-as-wall-street-expects-fight-with-revlon.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Gillette also bought back Revlon's stake in the company for $558 million.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Belkin |first1=Lisa |title=Gillette Deals Ends Revlon Bid |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1986/11/25/business/gillette-deal-ends-revlon-bid.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=November 25, 1986 |archive-date=June 16, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190616154927/https://www.nytimes.com/1986/11/25/business/gillette-deal-ends-revlon-bid.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Revlon made two additional unsolicited requests to purchase Gillette for $4.66 billion and $5.4 billion in June and August 1987, respectively, both of which were rejected by Gillette's board of directors.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ziegler |first1=Baart |title=Gillette Rejects Revlon Bid |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/business/1987/06/19/gillette-rejects-revlon-bid/4b12edb7-aa24-4bb4-98d8-6f1b3abf4282/ |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=June 19, 1987 |archive-date=September 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902005136/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/business/1987/06/19/gillette-rejects-revlon-bid/4b12edb7-aa24-4bb4-98d8-6f1b3abf4282/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jahn |first1=George |title=Gillette Rejects Third Takeover Bid By Revlon |url=https://apnews.com/article/335a5be71a7949e885901d8989fa411e |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=Associated Press |date=August 24, 1987}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cowan |first1=Alison Leigh |title=Revlon Asks to Bid For Gillette |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1987/08/18/business/revlon-asks-to-bid-for-gillette.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=August 18, 1987 |archive-date=November 5, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171105015858/http://www.nytimes.com/1987/08/18/business/revlon-asks-to-bid-for-gillette.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 1988, Coniston Partners acquired approximately 6 percent of Gillette.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cole |first1=Robert J. |title=Battle Begins For Control Of Gillette |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1988/02/12/business/battle-begins-for-control-of-gillette.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=February 12, 1988 |archive-date=January 30, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180130084630/http://www.nytimes.com/1988/02/12/business/battle-begins-for-control-of-gillette.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Hoping to acquire four directors' seats and pressure Gillette to sell, Coniston forced a proxy vote in April. The companies filed suits against one another, resulting in a settlement in August. Gillette repurchased approximately 16 million shares for $720 million and Coniston agreed not to purchase many Gillette shares, participate in proxy contests, or otherwise seek control of the company for three years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kennedy |first1=Dana |title=Gillette and Coniston Reach Settlement Over Proxy Fight |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/business/1988/08/02/gillette-and-coniston-reach-settlement-over-proxy-fight/18a77f86-e0f2-451b-9a3b-67e5539350a4/ |access-date=February 9, 2021 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=August 2, 1988 |archive-date=September 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902005129/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/business/1988/08/02/gillette-and-coniston-reach-settlement-over-proxy-fight/18a77f86-e0f2-451b-9a3b-67e5539350a4/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cowan |first1=Alison Leigh |title=Gillette and Coniston Drop Suits |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1988/08/02/business/gillette-and-coniston-drop-suits.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=August 2, 1988 |archive-date=May 25, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150525081026/http://www.nytimes.com/1988/08/02/business/gillette-and-coniston-drop-suits.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 1989, [[Warren Buffett]]'s [[Berkshire Hathaway]] purchased $600 million worth of Gillette convertible preferred shares. Buffett filled a vacant seat on the company's board and agreed not to sell his stake "except in a change of control or if insurance regulators force a sale of the stock in the event Gillette's financial condition falters", reducing the chances of a takeover.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cowan |first1=Alison Leigh |title=Gillette Sells 11% Stake To Buffett |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1989/07/21/business/gillette-sells-11-stake-to-buffett.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=July 21, 1989 |archive-date=December 19, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171219212823/http://www.nytimes.com/1989/07/21/business/gillette-sells-11-stake-to-buffett.html |url-status=live }}</ref> During late 1989 and early 1990, Gillette launched the new product Sensor with a $175 million marketing campaign in 19 countries in North America and Western Europe.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ramirez |first1=Anthony |title=Gillette Challenge to the Disposables |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1989/10/04/business/gillette-challenge-to-the-disposables.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=October 4, 1989 |archive-date=August 7, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807154809/https://www.nytimes.com/1989/10/04/business/gillette-challenge-to-the-disposables.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ramirez |first1=Anthony |title=A Radical New Style for Stodgy Old Gillette |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/02/25/business/a-radical-new-style-for-stodgy-old-gillette.html |access-date=February 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=February 25, 1990 |archive-date=September 4, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150904035425/http://www.nytimes.com/1990/02/25/business/a-radical-new-style-for-stodgy-old-gillette.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Gillette's attempt in 1990 to purchase [[Wilkinson Sword]]'s U.S. and non-European operations was blocked by the [[United States Department of Justice|Department of Justice]] to prevent a significant reduction in competition by eliminating one of the top four blade suppliers when Gillette already controlled approximately half of the nation's razor market.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rowley |first1=James |title=Justice Plans Antitrust Lawsuit to Halt Gillette-Wilkinson Deal |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/business/1990/01/10/justice-plans-antitrust-lawsuit-to-halt-gillette-wilkinson-deal/2bd28be9-dacb-45de-90b8-e7764507050d/ |access-date=February 11, 2021 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=January 10, 1990 |archive-date=August 27, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170827232817/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/business/1990/01/10/justice-plans-antitrust-lawsuit-to-halt-gillette-wilkinson-deal/2bd28be9-dacb-45de-90b8-e7764507050d/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Labaton |first1=Stephen |title=Gillette In Accord on Antitrust Suit |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/03/27/business/gillette-in-accord-on-antitrust-suit.html |access-date=February 11, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=March 27, 1990 |archive-date=May 25, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150525203457/http://www.nytimes.com/1990/03/27/business/gillette-in-accord-on-antitrust-suit.html |url-status=live }}</ref> 

The company launched [[Gillette Mach3]] in 1998, introducing a third blade to the existing successful twin-blade market. Gillette promoted the product, which took five years to develop and was protected by 35 patents, with a $300 million marketing campaign.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Canedy |first1=Dana |title=Gillette Unveils Its Mach3 Razor as Stock Backs Off |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1998/04/15/business/gillette-unveils-its-mach-3-razor-as-stock-backs-off.html |access-date=February 11, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=April 15, 1998 |archive-date=May 27, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527081820/http://www.nytimes.com/1998/04/15/business/gillette-unveils-its-mach-3-razor-as-stock-backs-off.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Gillette spends $750 million to make Mach3 Huge covert operation for new production line of three-blade razor |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/news/bs-xpm-1998-08-17-1998229063-story.html |access-date=February 24, 2021 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=August 17, 1998 |archive-date=June 21, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210621013551/https://www.baltimoresun.com/news/bs-xpm-1998-08-17-1998229063-story.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The Mach3 and replacement cartridges cost 35 percent more than the SensorExcel razor.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gillette sets Mach3 launch |url=https://money.cnn.com/1998/04/14/companies/gillette/ |access-date=February 11, 2021 |work=[[CNN Money]] |date=April 14, 1998 |archive-date=July 27, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190727193353/https://money.cnn.com/1998/04/14/companies/gillette/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> By 1999, Gillette was worth US$43&nbsp;billion, and the [[brand equity|brand value]] of Gillette was estimated to be worth US$16&nbsp;billion. This equated to 37% of the company's value.<ref>Jane Pavitt (2000). ''Brand New''. {{ISBN|069107061X}}{{page needed|date=September 2021}}</ref> In 2000, Gillette's board fired CEO Michael Hawley; he was replaced by former [[Nabisco]] CEO [[James M. Kilts]] in early 2001.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Herper |first1=Matthew |title=Focus On The Forbes 500s: Gillette Fires CEO |journal=[[Forbes]] |date=October 20, 2000 |url=https://www.forbes.com/2000/10/20/1020gillette.html?sh=5ef277987053 |access-date=February 24, 2021 |archive-date=September 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902005120/https://www.forbes.com/simple-data/native-ad/?feedId=6064abdac0d80c00429c1be2&pos=ntv-content |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Gillette taps Nabisco CEO |url=https://money.cnn.com/2001/01/22/companies/gillette/ |access-date=February 24, 2021 |work=[[CNN Money]] |date=January 22, 2001 |archive-date=February 25, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210225014830/https://money.cnn.com/2001/01/22/companies/gillette/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> In 2003, Schick-Wilkinson Sword introduced the Quattro, a four-blade shaving system, increasing the company's market share to 17 percent. Gillette claimed the Quattro infringed on the Mach3 patents.<ref name="Teather">{{cite news |last1=Teather |first1=David |title=It's Mach3 versus Quattro as Gillette crosses swords with Schick |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2003/aug/15/1 |access-date=February 24, 2021 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=August 14, 2003 |archive-date=August 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210826031917/https://www.theguardian.com/business/2003/aug/15/1 |url-status=live }}</ref> Gillette's efforts were unsuccessful, but the company maintained approximately two-thirds of the global wet-razor market as of mid-2005.<ref>{{cite journal |title=It's a cut-throat market |journal=[[Marketing Week]] |date=June 23, 2005 |url=https://www.marketingweek.com/its-a-cut-throat-market/ |access-date=February 24, 2021 |archive-date=August 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210826025937/https://www.marketingweek.com/its-a-cut-throat-market/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2005, [[Procter & Gamble]] announced plans to acquire Gillette for more than $50 billion, which would position P&G as the world's largest consumer products company.<ref>{{cite news |title=P&G Agrees to Buy Gillette In a $54 Billion Stock Deal |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB110693197048439468 |access-date=March 5, 2021 |work=The Wall Street Journal |date=January 30, 2005 |archive-date=April 11, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210411025811/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB110693197048439468 |url-status=live }}</ref> The deal was approved by the [[Federal Trade Commission]].<ref>{{cite journal |title=P&G Cleared For Gillette Buy |journal=Forbes |date=September 30, 2005 |url=https://www.forbes.com/2005/09/30/procter-gamble-gillette-cx_jl_0930video2.html?sh=6839b3a1160a |access-date=March 5, 2021 |archive-date=September 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902005135/https://geo.moatads.com/n.js?e=35&ol=0&qn=%604%7BZEYwoqI%24%5BK%2BdLLU)%2CMm~tB%23a.%5BMhS%3A15.sn_003etW6~P6Jn)s)wC%24GL3jX%7BQqDOJ%3Eoy)G3p%2FhFjrR8whh%2B%7D%407%25w_2C%3FP%3ElK%3DbH%2FH%40%26%2Bc%5B5IUOG(%2CWV%7BGrV~1HmDkP8D4rUDtmxT%3Bwv%40V374BKm55%3D%261fp%5BoU5tWhX%3C%3Ce%24%26~1%3Axkr%5BUe31k5X%5BG%5E%5B)%2C2iVSX%3C_Y%7B!7IQ3HbmUZzCFm%5Du!x2l.uBlTVU%2F.%3Dh%3FtDJq%409BG&tf=1_nMzjG---CSa7H-1SJH-bW7qhB-LRwqH-nMzjG-&vi=111111&rc=1%2C1%2C0%2C3%2C3326192205%2C1%2C4%2C0%2Cprobably%2Cprobably&rb=1-KvUj1TPl77BJmR4uHVjPGlx1P1tMeAYh8VIp1Vf7AbESGfrl5%2FmliBNlAlwWxmRnpyWz&sc=1&os=1-GQ%3D%3D&qp=10000&&iv=8&qt=0&gz=0&hh=0&hn=0&tw=Q)3E3iA&qc=10&qd=10&qf=1010&qe=19971&qh=1100&qg=900&qm=0&qa=1600&qb=1000&qi=1600&qj=1000&to=000&po=1-0020002000002120&vy=ot%24b%5Bh%40%22oD%7BMx5%3C1%3B(8.%7CLK2_v%5Eq%2BGs1%7B%2CyB%3AU!%2FoD%7BMx5%3C1%3B(Oy%2CUy%3CD&qr=0&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.forbes.com%2F2005%2F09%2F30%2Fprocter-gamble-gillette-cx_jl_0930video2.html%3Fsh%3D370d3e19160a&pcode=forbesprebidheader122641196143&ql=%3B%5BpwxnRd%7Dt%3Aal9EU%22y%2F.D%5B5%2F%5BGI%3Fi6%5EB61%2F%3DSqcMr1%7B%2CTu9LJJ(a.P%2B)s1(uA&qo=0&i=FORBES2&hp=1&zMoatGNID=7175&zMoatAU1=fdc.forbes&zMoatAU2=article-blue&zMoatAU3=standard&zMoatAU4=default&zMoatAU5=standard&wf=0&ra=1&pxm=3&sgs=3&vb=5&cm=9&kq=1&hq=0&hs=0&hu=0&hr=0&ht=1&dnt=0&bq=0&f=0&j=&t=1630543894520&de=87036814710&m=0&ar=503c7d4-clean&iw=92fc004&q=4&cb=0&ym=0&cu=1630543894520&ll=50&lm=0&ln=1&r=0&em=0&en=0&d=17094022%3A2347912927%3A5125792545%3A138344987118&zMoatSlotId=ntv-contentd&zMoatMMV=slotWaiting&zMoatMGV=slotWaiting&zMoatMSafety=safe&zMoatMData=1&zMoatCURL=forbes.com%2F2005%2F09%2F30%2Fprocter-gamble-gillette-cx_jl_0930video2.html&zMoatDev=Desktop&zMoatMMV_MAX=slotWaiting&zMoatMGV_MAX=slotWaiting&zMoatOrigSlicer1=105791542&zMoatOrigSlicer2=21919662559&zMoatSize=1x1&zMoatESChannel=investing%2Cmoney&zMoatESSection=investing%3Amarkets%2Cmoney%3Amarkets&zGSRS=1&zGSRC=1&gu=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.forbes.com%2F2005%2F09%2F30%2Fprocter-gamble-gillette-cx_jl_0930video2.html%3Fsh%3D370d3e19160a&id=1&ii=4&dfp=0%2C1&la=21919662559&bd=fdc.forbes%2Farticle_blue%2Fstandard%2Fdefault%2Fstandard&gw=forbes274355&fd=1&zMoatPos=ntv_contentd&zMoatZone=fdc.forbes%2Farticle_blue%2Fstandard%2Fdefault%2Fstandard&zMoatSpecialSlot=No%20Special%20Slot%20Defined&ac=1&it=500&ti=0&ih=1&pe=1%3A862%3A862%3A0%3A2594&tz=ntv-contentd&iq=slotWaiting&tt=slotWaiting&tu=1&tp=safe&jk=-1&jm=-1&fs=194337&na=1400132816&cs=0&callback=MoatDataJsonpRequest_48759512 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/press-releases/2005/09/ftc-consent-order-remedies-likely-anticompetitive-effects-procter|title=FTC Consent Order Remedies Likely Anticompetitive Effects of Procter & Gambles Acquisition of Gillette|date=September 30, 2005|publisher=[[Federal Trade Commission]]|access-date=March 6, 2021|archive-date=February 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210212054838/https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/press-releases/2005/09/ftc-consent-order-remedies-likely-anticompetitive-effects-procter|url-status=live}}</ref> Gillette introduced the world's first 5-blade razor, called the Fusion, during 2005–2006, marking the company's first launch after the P&G acquisition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gillette unveils 5-bladed razor |url=https://money.cnn.com/2005/09/14/news/fortune500/gillette/ |access-date=March 5, 2021 |work=CNN Money |publisher=CNN |date=September 14, 2005 |archive-date=February 27, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227120935/https://money.cnn.com/2005/09/14/news/fortune500/gillette/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> By 2010, the Fusion was the world's highest selling blade and razor brand, reaching $1 billion in annual sales faster than any prior P&G product.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wohl |first1=Jessica |title=Art of Shaving says new Gillette blade sales solid |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-procter-artofshaving-exclusive-idUSTRE64C3LE20100513 |access-date=March 4, 2021 |publisher=Reuters |date=May 13, 2010 |archive-date=January 6, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170106110630/http://www.reuters.com/article/us-procter-artofshaving-exclusive-idUSTRE64C3LE20100513 |url-status=live }}</ref> Gillette's Fusion ProSeries skincare line, launched in 2010, included a thermal facial scrub, a face wash, a lotion, and a moisturizer with [[sunscreen]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Evans |first1=Matthew W. |title=Gillette Launching Men's Fusion ProSeries |journal=[[Women's Wear Daily]] |date=March 9, 2010 |url=https://wwd.com/business-news/retail/gillette-launching-mens-fusion-proseries-2537127/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |archive-date=November 24, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201124191308/https://wwd.com/business-news/retail/gillette-launching-mens-fusion-proseries-2537127/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Baker |first1=Rosie |title=P&G launches Gillette Fusion ProGlide Series |journal=[[Marketing Week]] |date=February 12, 2010 |url=https://www.marketingweek.com/pg-launches-gillette-fusion-proglide-series/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |archive-date=December 1, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201201121915/https://www.marketingweek.com/pg-launches-gillette-fusion-proglide-series/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The Gillette Fusion ProGlide Styler for facial hair grooming was introduced in 2012, with [[André 3000]], [[Gael García Bernal]], and [[Adrien Brody]] serving as brand ambassadors.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Martin |first1=Peter |title=The New Shaver to Consider Using Today |journal=[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]] |date=February 14, 2021 |url=https://www.esquire.com/style/grooming/advice/a12804/gilette-fusion-proglide-styler-review-021412/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |archive-date=January 19, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210119024432/https://www.esquire.com/style/grooming/advice/a12804/gilette-fusion-proglide-styler-review-021412/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Gillette taps Benjamin, Brody and Bernal for ProGlide Styler ads |url=https://latimesblogs.latimes.com/alltherage/2012/01/gillette-taps-brody-benjamin-and-bernal-as-facial-hair-ambassadors.html |access-date=March 4, 2021 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=January 23, 2012 |archive-date=February 25, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160225130500/http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/alltherage/2012/01/gillette-taps-brody-benjamin-and-bernal-as-facial-hair-ambassadors.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2005, an [[injunction]] sought by rival [[Wilkinson Sword]] was granted by the [[Connecticut District Court]] which determined that Gillette's product claims were both "unsubstantiated and inaccurate" and that the [[product demonstration]]s in Gillette's advertising were "greatly exaggerated" and "literally false". While advertising in the United States had to be rewritten, the court's ruling does not apply in other countries.<ref>[http://www.gizmag.co.uk/go/4097 "Truth in advertising"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201226165653/https://newatlas.com/go/4097/ |date=2020-12-26 }}, Gizmag; accessed August 22, 2014.</ref>

In 2015, the company launched a subscription service called Gillette Shave Club{{NoteTag|Rebranded as Gillette On Demand in 2017<ref>{{cite news |last1=Green |first1=Dennis |title=Gillette just made an unprecedented change to be more like its competitors |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/gillette-launches-on-demand-razor-subscription-2017-5 |access-date=March 4, 2021 |work=[[Business Insider]] |date=May 9, 2017 |archive-date=September 18, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180918105336/https://www.businessinsider.com/gillette-launches-on-demand-razor-subscription-2017-5 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Thomas |first1=Lauren |title=Gillette one ups Dollar Shave Club with on-demand razor ordering service where you text to order |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2017/05/09/gillette-one-ups-dollar-shave-club-with-on-demand-razor-ordering-service-where-you-text-to-order.html |access-date=March 4, 2021 |publisher=[[CNBC]] |date=May 9, 2017 |archive-date=January 25, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210125190640/https://www.cnbc.com/2017/05/09/gillette-one-ups-dollar-shave-club-with-on-demand-razor-ordering-service-where-you-text-to-order.html |url-status=live }}</ref>}} and later filed a lawsuit against [[Dollar Shave Club]] for patent infringement.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gillette files patent lawsuit against Dollar Shave Club |url=https://www.denverpost.com/2015/12/17/gillette-files-patent-lawsuit-against-dollar-shave-club/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |work=[[The Denver Post]] |date=December 17, 2015 |archive-date=April 30, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170430004751/http://www.denverpost.com/2015/12/17/gillette-files-patent-lawsuit-against-dollar-shave-club/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Harwell |first1=Drew |title=Gillette's lawsuit could tilt the battle for America's beards |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/business/wp/2015/12/18/gillettes-lawsuit-could-tilt-the-battle-for-americas-beards/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=December 18, 2015 |archive-date=December 19, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151219173859/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/business/wp/2015/12/18/gillettes-lawsuit-could-tilt-the-battle-for-americas-beards/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The Dollar Shave Club lawsuit was criticized for revealing flaws in Gillette's own patents<ref name="cnbclawsuit">{{cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2015/12/18/heres-how-dollar-shave-can-beat-gillettes-lawsuit.html|title=The nick in Gillette suit against upstart Dollar Shave Club|work=CNBC|last=Spiegel|first=David|date=18 December 2015|access-date=6 March 2021|archive-date=18 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200918231940/https://www.cnbc.com/2015/12/18/heres-how-dollar-shave-can-beat-gillettes-lawsuit.html|url-status=live}}</ref> and as a perceived attempt to drive away an upstart competitor; the lawsuit was dropped two months later after Dollar Shave Club filed a countersuit.<ref name="w3">{{cite web|url=https://w3-lab.com/gillettes-ad-dollar-shave-club/|title=How Gillette's Ad Helped Dollar Shave Club|work=W3 Labs|last=Milosavljević|first=Marija|access-date=6 March 2021|archive-date=20 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210120165357/https://w3-lab.com/gillettes-ad-dollar-shave-club/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Gillette was fined by [[Autorité de la concurrence]] in France in 2016 for [[price fixing]] on personal hygiene products.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.connexionfrance.com/court-appeal-price-fixing-fine-upheld-consumer-companies-gillette-loreal-procter-gamble-sc-johnson-18567-view-article.html |title=Huge price-fixing fine is upheld |publisher=The Connexion |date=28 October 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20170209165431/http://www.connexionfrance.com/court-appeal-price-fixing-fine-upheld-consumer-companies-gillette-loreal-procter-gamble-sc-johnson-18567-view-article.html |archive-date=9 February 2017 }}</ref>

In January 2019, Gillette began a new marketing campaign, "[[The Best Men Can Be]]", to mark the 30th anniversary of the "Best a Man Can Get" slogan. The campaign was introduced with a long-form commercial entitled "We Believe", and aimed to promote positive [[values]] among men – condemning acts of [[bullying]], [[sexism]], [[sexual assault]], and [[toxic masculinity]]. While the campaign received praise for its acknowledgement of current [[social movement]]s and for promoting positive values of [[masculinity]], it also faced a negative response – including from [[Conservatism in the United States|right-wing]] critics<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nationalreview.com/2019/01/gillette-ad-conservative-message-on-masculinity/|title=Gillette's Ad Is More Conservative Than Critics Think|date=2019-01-17|website=National Review|language=en-US|access-date=2019-01-17|archive-date=2020-12-23|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201223064137/https://www.nationalreview.com/2019/01/gillette-ad-conservative-message-on-masculinity/|url-status=live}}</ref> – being called [[left-wing]] propaganda, accusatory towards its customers, and [[misandrist]] and there were calls for boycotts of Gillette.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-01-16/gillette-toxic-masculinity-advertisement-courts-talk-of-boycotts/10718004|title=Gillette faces talks of boycott over ad campaign railing against toxic masculinity|date=2019-01-16|website=ABC News|language=en-AU|access-date=2019-01-16|archive-date=2020-11-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108033639/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-01-16/gillette-toxic-masculinity-advertisement-courts-talk-of-boycotts/10718004|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/gillette-metoo-commercial-criticizes-men-2019-1|title=Gillette chastises men in a new commercial highlighting the #MeToo movement – and some are furious|last=Green|first=Dennis|date=2019-01-14|work=[[Business Insider]]|access-date=2019-01-14|archive-date=2020-10-31|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031063114/https://www.businessinsider.com/gillette-metoo-commercial-criticizes-men-2019-1|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.boston.com/news/business/2019/01/14/gillette-ad-men-me-too/|title=Gillette released an ad asking men to 'act the right way.' Then came the backlash.|date=2019-01-14|work=Boston.com|access-date=2019-01-15|language=en-US|archive-date=2020-12-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201225224206/https://www.boston.com/news/business/2019/01/14/gillette-ad-men-me-too|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="adweek-bestmencanbe" /><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://adage.com/article/cmo-strategy/gillette-s-a-man-responsibility/316231/|title=Gillette's new take on 'Best a Man Can Get' in commercial that invokes #MeToo|work=[[Advertising Age]]|date=14 January 2019|language=en|access-date=2019-01-15|archive-date=2020-12-24|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201224075350/https://adage.com/article/cmo-strategy/gillette-s-a-man-responsibility/316231|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2019, the company partnered with [[TerraCycle]] to create a U.S. recycling program for blades, razors, and packaging for any brand.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Peters |first1=Adele |title=You can now send Gillette your old razors to have them recycled |journal=[[Fast Company]] |date=March 15, 2019 |url=https://www.fastcompany.com/90320841/you-can-now-send-gillette-your-old-razors-to-have-them-recycled |access-date=March 4, 2021 |archive-date=April 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210410235612/https://www.fastcompany.com/90320841/you-can-now-send-gillette-your-old-razors-to-have-them-recycled |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2020, Gillette announced a commitment to reduce the use of virgin (unrecycled) plastics by 50 percent by 2030 and maintain zero waste to landfill status across all plants.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Anderson |first1=Deonna |title=Gillette plans to shave use of virgin (unrecycled) plastics by 50% by 2030 |url=https://www.greenbiz.com/article/gillette-plans-shave-use-virgin-plastics-50-2030 |website=GreenBiz |access-date=March 4, 2021 |date=October 27, 2020 |archive-date=February 12, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210212075013/https://www.greenbiz.com/article/gillette-plans-shave-use-virgin-plastics-50-2030 |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Product history ==
{{More citations needed section|date=February 2017}}

=== Double-edged safety razors ===
[[File:1930's Gillette Razor and Packaging.tif|thumb|Gillette razor and packaging, {{c.|1930s}}]]
The first [[safety razor]] using the new [[disposable]] blade went on sale in 1903.<ref name=":0" /> Gillette maintained a limited range of models of this new type razor until 1921 when the original Gillette patent expired.<ref name=":1" /> In anticipation of the event, Gillette introduced a redesigned razor and offered it at a variety of prices in different cases and finishes, including the long-running "aristocrat". Gillette continued to sell the original razor but instead of pricing it at $5, it was priced at $1, making a Gillette razor truly affordable to every man regardless of economic class. In 1932 the Gillette Blue Blade, so-named because it was dipped in blue lacquer, was introduced. It became one of the most recognizable blades in the world. In 1934 the "twist to open" (TTO) design was instituted, which featured butterfly-like doors that made blade changing much easier than it had been, wherein the razor head had to be detached from the handle.

Razor handles continued to advance to allow consumers to achieve a closer shave. In 1947, the new (TTO) model, the ''"Super Speed"'', was introduced. This was updated in 1955, with different versions being produced to shave more closely – the degree of closeness being marked by the color of the handle tip.

In 1955, the first ''adjustable'' razor was produced. This allowed for an adjustment of the blade to increase the closeness of the shave. The model, in various versions, remained in production until 1988.<ref>{{cite web|title=Vintage Gillette Adjustable Double Edge Razors|url=http://www.gilletteadjustable.com|publisher=GilletteAdjustable.com|access-date=12 March 2017|archive-date=24 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171224055051/http://www.gilletteadjustable.com/|url-status=live}}</ref>[[File:Vintage Gillette Safety Razor, Old Type Set, Made In USA, Circa 1920s (19844414006).jpg|thumb|"Old type" Gillette safety razor, made between 1921 and 1928]]

The Super Speed razor was again redesigned in 1966 and given a black resin coated metal handle. It remained in production until 1988. A companion model the, ''"Knack"'', with a longer plastic handle, was produced from 1966 to 1975. In Europe, the Knack was sold as "Slim Twist" and "G2000" from 1978 to 1988, a later version known as "G1000" was made in England and available until 1998. A modern version of the Tech, with a plastic thin handle, is still produced and sold in several countries under the names 7 O'clock, Gillette, Nacet, Minora, Rubie, and Economica.

=== Discontinued products ===
* '''Techmatic''' was a single blade razor introduced in the mid-1960s. It featured a disposable cartridge with a razor band which was advanced by means of a lever. This exposed an unused portion of the band and was the equivalent of five blades. This product line also included the Adjustable Techmatic.
* '''Trac II''' was the world's first two-blade razor, debuting in 1971.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.vox.com/the-goods/2018/12/11/18134456/best-razor-gillette-harrys-dollar-shave-club|title=The absurd quest to make the "best" razor|last=Tiffany|first=Kaitlyn|work=Vox|access-date=2019-09-18|archive-date=2020-10-31|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031110841/https://www.vox.com/the-goods/2018/12/11/18134456/best-razor-gillette-harrys-dollar-shave-club|url-status=live}}</ref> Gillette claimed that the second blade cut the number of strokes required and reduced facial irritation. This product line also included the Trac II Plus.
* '''Atra''' (known as the ''Contour'', ''Slalom'', ''Vector'' in some markets) was introduced in 1977<ref name=":1" /> and was the first razor to feature a pivoting head, which Gillette claimed made it easier for men to shave their necks. This product line also included the Atra Plus, which featured a lubricating strip, dubbed ''Lubra-Soft''.
* '''Good News!''' was the first disposable, double-blade razor, released in 1976.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cutler |first1=Rodney |title=The Grooming Awards Hall of Fame: Gillette Good News |url=https://www.esquire.com/style/grooming/a3382/goodnews1007/ |access-date=January 7, 2021 |work=[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]] |date=September 11, 2007 |archive-date=February 24, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210224233437/https://www.esquire.com/style/grooming/a3382/goodnews1007/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Tobias |first1=Andrew |title=Gillette's New Ploy: Throwing Away the Razors to Sell the Blades |date=July 12, 1976 |publisher=New York Media, LLC |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5uQCAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA55 |access-date=January 7, 2021 |volume=9 |issue=28 |page=55 |issn=0028-7369 |journal=[[New York (magazine)|New York]] |archive-date=September 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902005119/https://books.google.com/books?id=5uQCAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA55 |url-status=live }}</ref> Varieties included the "Original", the "Good News! Plus", and the "Good News! Pivot Plus".
* '''Custom Plus''' was a series of disposable razors that came in many varieties: the "Fixed Disposable razor", the "Pivot Disposable razor", the "Custom Plus 3 Sensitive Disposable", and the "Custom Plus 3 Soothing Disposable". '''Fusion Power Phenom''' was released in February 2008. It had a blue and silver color scheme.<ref name="Gillette">{{cite press release|url=http://www.pginvestor.com/phoenix.zhtml?c=104574&p=irol-newsArticle&ID=1106705&highlight= |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120907022615/http://www.pginvestor.com/phoenix.zhtml?c=104574&p=irol-newsArticle&ID=1106705&highlight= |url-status=dead |archive-date=September 7, 2012 |title=Gillette Unveils Newest Members of Its Gillette Young Guns Lineup |publisher=Gillette |date=February 11, 2008 |access-date=February 15, 2008 }}</ref> Other discontinued variants include the Fusion Power Gamer.
* '''Body''' was a 3 blade razor in care of the man's body introduced in 2014. However, the Gillette Body was discontinued in 2018.

=== Current products ===
* '''Gillette Sensor''' debuted in 1990,<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-manufacturing-respect-idUSTRE7BD1WR20111214|title=Manufacturing can thrive but struggles for respect|date=2011-12-14|work=Reuters|access-date=2019-09-18|language=en|archive-date=2020-08-09|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200809070223/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-manufacturing-respect-idUSTRE7BD1WR20111214|url-status=live}}</ref> and was designed by [[Dieter Rams]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://arun.is/blog/gillette/|title=Dieter Rams designed one of Gillette's most successful razors |date=2019-08-12 |website=arun.is/blog/gillette |access-date=2024-03-22 }}</ref> It was the first razor to have spring-loaded blades. Gillette claimed the blades receded into the cartridge head, when they make contact with skin, helping to prevent cuts and allowing for a closer shave.<ref name="Boston">{{cite news |title=Gillette Ups the Razor Ante. Again. |url=https://www.boston.com/news/untagged/2014/04/29/gillette-ups-the-razor-ante-again/ |access-date=January 7, 2021 |work=[[Boston.com]] |date=April 29, 2014 |archive-date=January 28, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128132545/https://www.boston.com/news/omg/2014/04/29/gillette-ups-the-razor-ante-again |url-status=live }}</ref> This product line also includes the Sensor Excel,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Shoebridge |first1=Neil |title=Gillette's better-blade plan has profits rising smoothly |url=https://www.afr.com/companies/gillettes-better-blade-plan-has-profits-rising-smoothly-19951023-kayh8 |access-date=January 7, 2021 |work=[[Australian Financial Review]] |date=October 23, 1995 |archive-date=January 29, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210129072822/https://www.afr.com/companies/gillettes-better-blade-plan-has-profits-rising-smoothly-19951023-kayh8 |url-status=live }}</ref> the Sensor 3, and the Sensor 3 Cool.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Selwood|first1=Daniel|date=February 20, 2018|title=Gillette counters falling sales with packaging overhaul|url=https://www.thegrocer.co.uk/marketing/gillette-counters-falling-sales-with-packaging-overhaul/563566.article|journal=[[The Grocer]]|access-date=January 20, 2021|archive-date=January 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210129193020/https://www.thegrocer.co.uk/marketing/gillette-counters-falling-sales-with-packaging-overhaul/563566.article|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Utroske|first1=Deanna|date=March 27, 2018|title=P&G launches 5 new Gillette razors to keep pace with men's grooming preferences|url=https://www.cosmeticsdesign.com/Article/2018/03/27/P-G-launches-5-new-Gillette-razors-to-keep-pace-with-men-s-grooming-preferences|access-date=January 20, 2021|website=Cosmetics Design|archive-date=October 29, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201029142440/https://www.cosmeticsdesign.com/Article/2018/03/27/P-G-launches-5-new-Gillette-razors-to-keep-pace-with-men-s-grooming-preferences|url-status=live}}</ref>
* '''Blue II''' is a line of disposable razors, rebranding the old brand '''Good News!'''. In Latin America, it was marketed as the ''Prestobarba''. Another product in the line is the Blue 3, a line of three-blade disposable razors, cheaper than Sensor 3.
* '''[[Gillette Mach 3|Mach3]]''' – the first three-blade razor, introduced in 1998,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.vox.com/the-goods/2018/12/11/18134456/best-razor-gillette-harrys-dollar-shave-club|title=The absurd quest to make the "best" razor|last=Tiffany|first=Kaitlyn|date=2018-12-11|website=Vox|access-date=2019-09-18|archive-date=2020-10-31|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031110841/https://www.vox.com/the-goods/2018/12/11/18134456/best-razor-gillette-harrys-dollar-shave-club|url-status=live}}</ref> which Gillette claims reduces irritation and requires fewer strokes. In 2016,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2016/07/25/p-g-upgrades-gillette-razor-for-first-time-in.html|title=P&G upgrades Gillette razor for first time in nearly a decade|last=Brunsman|first=Barrett|website=Cincinnati Business Courier|access-date=2019-09-18|archive-date=2016-07-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160726144900/http://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2016/07/25/p-g-upgrades-gillette-razor-for-first-time-in.html|url-status=live}}</ref> P&G upgraded the Gillette Mach3 razor: This product comes in disposable, sensitive and "turbo" variants as well.<ref name="TrendSpotter">{{cite web |last1=Day |first1=Eliza |title=10 Best Razors for Men Who Like a Close Shave |url=https://www.thetrendspotter.net/best-razors-for-men/ |website=The Trend Spotter |date=9 May 2020 |access-date=January 7, 2021 |archive-date=31 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201231041325/https://www.thetrendspotter.net/best-razors-for-men/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="BestManualRazor">{{cite news |title=The Best Men's Razors (for Any Face) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/wirecutter/reviews/best-manual-razor/ |access-date=January 7, 2021 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=December 21, 2020 |first1=Dan |last1=Koeppel |first2=Justin |last2=Redman |archive-date=January 4, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210104185040/https://www.nytimes.com/wirecutter/reviews/best-manual-razor/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
* '''Venus''' is a division of razors for women created in 2000. Products include Venus Divine, Venus Vibrance, Venus Embrace, Venus Breeze, Venus Spa Breeze and Venus ProSkin Moisture Rich.
* '''Gillette Fusion''' is a five-bladed razor released in 2006. The Fusion has five blades on the front and a single sixth blade on the rear for precision trimming.<ref>{{cite magazine|last1=Null|first1=Christopher|date=October 23, 2007|title=Gillette Fusion Power Phantom|url=https://www.wired.com/2007/10/hh-gillette-fusion-power/|magazine=[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]]|access-date=January 21, 2021|archive-date=January 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210129011651/https://www.wired.com/2007/10/hh-gillette-fusion-power/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=February 16, 2007|title=Gillette Fusion Power Phantom|url=https://www.thrillist.com/node/18240|access-date=January 21, 2021|website=[[Thrillist]]|archive-date=January 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210129102549/https://www.thrillist.com/node/18240|url-status=live}}</ref> Its marketing campaign was fronted by the sports stars [[Roger Federer]], [[Thierry Henry]], and [[Tiger Woods]].<ref>{{cite news|date=April 29, 2014|title=Gillette Ups the Razor Ante. Again.|work=[[Boston.com]]|url=https://www.boston.com/news/untagged/2014/04/29/gillette-ups-the-razor-ante-again/|access-date=January 7, 2021|archive-date=January 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128132545/https://www.boston.com/news/omg/2014/04/29/gillette-ups-the-razor-ante-again|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Gilbert|first1=Sarah|title=Did Gillette's Fusion ad doom Tiger Woods?|date=15 July 2016 |url=https://www.aol.com/2009/12/10/could-gillettes-fusion-ad-have-doomed-tiger-woods/|access-date=January 21, 2021|publisher=[[AOL]]|archive-date=January 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128194238/https://www.aol.com/2009/12/10/could-gillettes-fusion-ad-have-doomed-tiger-woods/|url-status=live}}</ref> Razors in this product line include Fusion Power, Fusion ProGlide Shield, Fusion ProGlide, Fusion ProGlide Power,<ref>{{cite news|last1=Byron|first1=Ellen|date=February 12, 2010|title=P&G Razor Launches in Recession's Shadow|work=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748704337004575059760286648800|access-date=January 21, 2021|archive-date=January 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128104117/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748704337004575059760286648800|url-status=live}}</ref> and Fusion ProGlide with FlexBall Technology.<ref name="Gillette"/><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2014/05/01/gillette-hopes-for-a-swivel-in-sales-from-new-razor.html|title=Gillette hopes for a swivel in sales from new razor|last=Eisen|first=Sara|date=2014-05-01|website=CNBC|language=en|access-date=2019-10-23|archive-date=2020-11-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125030533/https://www.cnbc.com/2014/05/01/gillette-hopes-for-a-swivel-in-sales-from-new-razor.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The ProGlide FlexBall has a handle that allows the razor cartridge to pivot.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=http://nymag.com/intelligencer/2014/04/gillettes-razor-everything-wrong-with-america.html|title=Gillette's New Razor Is Everything That's Wrong With American Innovation|website=Intelligencer|date=18 April 2014 |language=en-us|access-date=2019-10-23|archive-date=2020-11-12|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112025212/http://nymag.com/intelligencer/2014/04/gillettes-razor-everything-wrong-with-america.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
* '''Gillette All-Purpose Styler''', released in 2012, is a waterproof beard trimmer that can cut four different lengths.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Foster |first1=Megan |title=The best 22 grooming gifts for dad |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/shopping/gift-guides/best-grooming-gifts-dad-fathers-day-n1214801 |publisher=NBC News |date=June 3, 2020 |accessdate=May 5, 2021 |archive-date=May 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210510203231/https://www.nbcnews.com/shopping/gift-guides/best-grooming-gifts-dad-fathers-day-n1214801 |url-status=live }}</ref>
* '''Treo''' is the first razor designed for caregivers to shave seniors and people with disabilities, was introduced in 2017.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Brunsman |first1=Barrett J. |title=P&G rolls out first-of-its-kind razor |journal=Cincinnati Business Courier |date=October 17, 2017 |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2017/10/17/p-g-rolls-out-razor-designed-for-those-who-can-t.html |access-date=March 4, 2021 |archive-date=September 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902005142/https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2017/10/17/p-g-rolls-out-razor-designed-for-those-who-can-t.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The product was named one of the best inventions of 2018 by ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'',<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Cooney |first1=Samantha |title=Best Inventions: A Razor Built for Assisted Shaving: Gillette Treo |magazine=Time |url=https://time.com/collection/best-inventions-2018/5454217/gillette-treo/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |archive-date=March 12, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210312045941/https://time.com/collection/best-inventions-2018/5454217/gillette-treo/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and later exhibited at the [[Consumer Electronics Show]] (CES) in 2020.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Takahashi |first1=Dean |title=How tech is catering to the elderly and caregivers |url=https://venturebeat.com/2020/01/13/how-the-tech-industry-is-paying-attention-to-elderly-consumers-and-caregivers/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |work=[[VentureBeat]] |date=January 13, 2020 |archive-date=March 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302201955/https://venturebeat.com/2020/01/13/how-the-tech-industry-is-paying-attention-to-elderly-consumers-and-caregivers/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Brunsman |first1=Barrett J. |title=P&G begins selling first razor for people who can't shave themselves |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2019/08/09/p-g-begins-selling-first-razor-for-people-who-can.html |access-date=March 4, 2021 |work=Cincinnati Business Courier |date=August 9, 2019 |archive-date=September 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210902005120/https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2019/08/09/p-g-begins-selling-first-razor-for-people-who-can.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
* '''SkinGuard''' is a razor designed for people with sensitive skin, introduced during 2018–2019.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Brunsman |first1=Barrett J. |title=I Tried It: P&G's new razor the best from Gillette yet |journal=[[Cincinnati Business Courier]] |date=December 18, 2018 |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2018/12/18/i-tried-it-p-g-s-new-razor-the-best-from-gillette.html |access-date=March 4, 2021 |quote=Procter & Gamble Co.’s new Gillette SkinGuard razor for men with sensitive skin has begun hitting store shelves and will continue rolling out in the U.S. and Europe into early 2019, backed by a heavy investment in advertising. |archive-date=December 30, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201230000343/https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2018/12/18/i-tried-it-p-g-s-new-razor-the-best-from-gillette.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
* In 2019, the company launched the first heated razor, mimicking a hot towel shave.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Meyersohn |first1=Nathaniel |title=Gillette is selling a $200 luxury razor that heats up to 122 degrees |url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/05/02/tech/gillette-heated-razor/index.html |access-date=March 4, 2021 |work=[[CNN Business]] |publisher=CNN |date=May 2, 2019 |archive-date=January 8, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210108232238/https://www.cnn.com/2019/05/02/tech/gillette-heated-razor/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The prototype was showcased at CES and later named one of the best inventions of 2019 by ''Time''.<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Lang |first1=Cady |title=Best Inventions: A Closer Shave: Heated Razor by GilletteLabs |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |url=https://time.com/collection/best-inventions-2019/5733057/heated-razor-by-gillettelabs/ |access-date=March 4, 2021 |archive-date=January 22, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210122171004/https://time.com/collection/best-inventions-2019/5733057/heated-razor-by-gillettelabs/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The heated razor received mixed reviews, with reviewers, including professional barbers, describing the design as flawed and the $200 price tag as overpriced.<ref name="heatedreview1">{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/wirecutter/blog/gillette-heated-razor/|title=We Tried the Gillette Heated Razor. It's Not So Hot.|work=[[The New York Times]]|last=Koeppel|first=Dan|date=5 July 2019|access-date=6 March 2021|archive-date=2 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210202104745/https://www.nytimes.com/wirecutter/blog/gillette-heated-razor/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="heatedreview2">{{cite web|url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/business/2019/05/12/need-spark-gillette-brings-heat/kqHHYFA5NFy4FkVxipqTyH/story.html|title=In need of a spark, Gillette brings the heat|work=[[The Boston Globe]]|last=Bray|first=Hiawatha|date=12 May 2019|access-date=6 March 2021|archive-date=2 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191102052711/https://www.bostonglobe.com/business/2019/05/12/need-spark-gillette-brings-heat/kqHHYFA5NFy4FkVxipqTyH/story.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
* '''King C. Gillette Beard Care Line''' came out in May 2020 named for the company's founder and offers beard care products including a Complete Men's Beard Kit, a double-sided safety razor, shave gel, beard and face wash, balm and oil.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kumar |first1=Naveen |title=18 beard grooming products that experts love |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/04/21/cnn-underscored/best-beard-grooming-kits/index.html |access-date=May 5, 2021 |publisher=CNN |date=May 5, 2021 |archive-date=May 11, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210511115623/https://www.cnn.com/2021/04/21/cnn-underscored/best-beard-grooming-kits/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
* '''Planet KIND Skincare Line''' launched in February 2021 as a new sustainable shaving and skincare brand, featuring a razor, shave cream, moisturizer and face wash. The razor is constructed from 60 percent recycled plastic and refills are designed with five blades that can each be used for up to a month. Planet KIND partnered with recycling company TerraCycle to design a program through which you can recycle the razor and blades.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Malin |first1=Zoe |title=New & Notable: Latest products from Nike, Old Navy and more |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/shopping/new-notable/new-products-nike-old-navy-gillette-n1263027 |access-date=May 5, 2021 |publisher=NBC News |date=April 5, 2021 |archive-date=May 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210510203231/https://www.nbcnews.com/shopping/new-notable/new-products-nike-old-navy-gillette-n1263027 |url-status=live }}</ref>

